\label{lem:single-site-dynkin}
Let $(X_t)_{t\geqslant0}$ denote the trajectory of Glauber dynamics for the Hamiltonian $V$ at inverse temperature $\beta$. For any $f:\Omega_N\to\mathbb{R}$, the process
\begin{align*}
    M_t^f=f(X_t)-f(X_0)-\int_0^t\mathcal{L}_{V,\beta}f(X_s)\,ds
\end{align*}
is a square integrable martingale with $M_0^f=0$, known as the Dynkin martingale. Its predictable quadratic variation is
\begin{align}
\label{eq:single-site-predictable-bracket}
    \langle M^f\rangle_t
    =2\int_0^t\Gamma_{V,\beta}(f,f)(X_s)\,ds,
\end{align}
where $\Gamma_{V,\beta}$ denotes the carr\'e du champ operator associated with $\mathcal{L}_{V,\beta}$, defined by
\begin{align*}
    \Gamma_{V,\beta}(f,g)(x)
    \doteq\frac12\bigl[\mathcal{L}_{V,\beta}(fg)-f\mathcal{L}_{V,\beta}g-g\mathcal{L}_{V,\beta}f\bigr](x)
    =2\sum_{i=1}^N c_i^{(V,\beta)}(x)\partial_i f(x)\partial_i g(x).
\end{align*}

