{\mathcal{D}^1}\leqslant2$ by \cref{lem:overlap-sobolev-bounds} and $\nabla_\Omega m=x^{\star}/N$, we infer from the Markov property, \cref{lem:dynkin-martingale-concentration}, and a union bound that
\begin{align}
\label{eq:strong-recovery-martingale-bound}
    \mathbb{P}[\mathcal M^c]
    \leqslant\sum_{j=0}^{\lceil T\rceil-1}\mathbb{P}[\mathcal M_j^c]
    \leqslant2\lceil T\rceil\exp\left(-cN\min\{\bar r_N^2,q_N^2\}\right).
\end{align}
Henceforth, we work on the event $\mathcal{E}\cap\mathcal M$.

We first express the deficit $1-m(X_t)$ in terms of $b(X_t)$. By \cref{eq:strong-recovery-overlap-drift,eq:strong-recovery-dynkin},
\begin{align*}
    1-m(X_t)+M_t^m
    =1-m(X_0)-\int_0^t\bigl[1-m(X_s)-2b(X_s)\bigr]\,ds.
\end{align*}
Since the integrand is bounded and measurable, the right-hand side is Lipschitz in $t$, and thus differentiable almost everywhere. Hence, for almost every $t\geqslant0$,
\begin{align*}
    \frac{d}{dt}\Bigl[e^t\bigl(1-m(X_t)+M_t^m\bigr)\Bigr]
    &=e^t\bigl(1-m(X_t)+M_t^m\bigr)
      +e^t\,\frac{d}{dt}\bigl(1-m(X_t)+M_t^m\bigr)\\
    &=e^t\bigl(1-m(X_t)+M_t^m\bigr)-e^t\bigl[1-m(X_t)-2b(X_t)\bigr]\\
    &=e^t\bigl[M_t^m+2b(X_t)\bigr].
\end{align*}
Integrating this identity from $0$ to $t$ and using $M_0^m=0$, we obtain
\begin{align*}
    e^t\bigl(1-m(X_t)+M_t^m\bigr)
    =1-m(X_0)+\int_0^te^s\bigl[M_s^m+2b(X_s)\bigr]\,ds.
\end{align*}
Multiplying both sides by $e^{-t}$ and rearranging, we conclude that
\begin{align*}
    1-m(X_t)
    =e^{-t}\bigl(1-m(X_0)\bigr)
     +\int_0^te^{-(t-s)}\,2b(X_s)\,ds
     +\int_0^te^{-(t-s)}M_s^m\,ds-M_t^m.
\end{align*}
Since we work on $\mathcal M$, we infer from the triangle inequality that
\begin{align*}
    \bigl|M_t^m-M_s^m\bigr|
    &\leqslant\bigl|M_t^m-M_{\lfloor t\rfloor}^m\bigr|
      +\sum_{l=\lfloor s\rfloor+1}^{\lfloor t\rfloor}\bigl|M_l^m-M_{l-1}^m\bigr|
      +\bigl|M_s^m-M_{\lfloor s\rfloor}^m\bigr|\\
    &\leqslant\bigl(\lfloor t\rfloor-\lfloor s\rfloor+2\bigr)\varepsilon_N
    \leqslant(t-s+3)\varepsilon_N,
    \qquad0\leqslant s\leqslant t\leqslant T.
\end{align*}
Since $M_0^m=0$ and $\int_0^te^{-(t-s)}\,ds=1-e^{-t}$, the last two terms satisfy
\begin{align*}
    \left|\int_0^te^{-(t-s)}M_s^m\,ds-M_t^m\right|
    &=\left|\int_0^te^{-(t-s)}\bigl(M_s^m-M_t^m\bigr)\,ds-e^{-t}\bigl(M_t^m-M_0^m\bigr)\right|\\
    &\leqslant\varepsilon_N\int_0^te^{-(t-s)}(t-s+3)\,ds+\varepsilon_Ne^{-t}(t+3)
    \leqslant7\varepsilon_N,
    \qquad0\leqslant t\leqslant T,
\end{align*}
where the last inequality uses $\int_0^\infty e^{-u}(u+3)\,du=4$ and $e^{-t}(t+3)\leqslant3$. Consequently,
\begin{align}
\label{eq:strong-recovery-deficit-bound}
    1-m(X_t)
    \leqslant e^{-t}\bigl(1-m(X_0)\bigr)+\int_0^te^{-(t-s)}\,2b(X_s)\,ds+7\varepsilon_N,
    \qquad0\leqslant t\leqslant T.
\end{align}

We next bound $b(x)$ in terms of the correlation $m(x)$. Since $H=N\lambda m^k+H_0$, we have $x^{\star}_i\partial_iH(x)=N\lambdax^{\star}_i\partial_i(m^k)(x)+x^{\star}_i\partial_iH_0(x)$ for every $i\in[N]$. Define $m_{-i}(x)\doteq N^{-1}\sum_{j\ne i}x^{\star}_jx_j$. If $m(x)\geqslant4/N$, then $m_{-i}(x)\pm1/N\geqslant m(x)-2/N\geqslant m(x)/2$. Hence, by the definition of the discrete partial derivative and the mean value theorem,
\begin{align}
\label{eq:strong-recovery-signal-field}
    N\lambdax^{\star}_i\partial_i(m^k)(x)
    &=\frac{N\lambda}{2}\left[
        \left(m_{-i}(x)+\frac1N\right)^k
        -\left(m_{-i}(x)-\frac1N\right)^k
    \right]\nonumber\\
    &\geqslant k\lambda\left(\frac{m(x)}2\right)^{k-1}
    \geqslant c_k\lambda m(x)^{k-1}.
\end{align}
Let $\mathcal{I}(x)\doteq\{i\in[N]:x^{\star}_i\partial_iH_0(x)\leqslant-\tfrac{c_k}{2}\lambda m(x)^{k-1}\}$. Since we work on $\mathcal{E}$,
\begin{align*}
    |\mathcal{I}(x)|\,\frac{c_k^2\lambda^2m(x)^{2k-2}}{4}
    \leqslant\sum_{i\in\mathcal{I}(x)}\bigl(\partial_iH_0(x)\bigr)^2
    \leqslant 
    \IfNoValueTF{^}
        {\IfBooleanTF{\nabla_\Omega H_0(x)}{\normdelim{#3}}{\normdelim*{#3}}}
        {\normdelim[^]{#3}} 
2
    \leqslant C_1N.
\end{align*}
Moreover, for every $i\notin\mathcal{I}(x)$, we infer from \cref{eq:strong-recovery-signal-field} that $x^{\star}_i\partial_iH(x)>\tfrac{c_k}{2}\lambda m(x)^{k-1}$, and therefore
\begin{align*}
    \frac{1-\tanh\bigl(\betax^{\star}_i\partial_iH(x)\bigr)}{2}
    =\frac{1}{1+\exp\bigl(2\betax^{\star}_i\partial_iH(x)\bigr)}
    \leqslant\exp\bigl(-c_k\beta\lambda m(x)^{k-1}\bigr).
\end{align*}
Bounding the same quantity by $1$ for $i\in\mathcal{I}(x)$, we conclude that the following holds whenever $m(x)\geqslant4/N$:
\begin{align}
\label{eq:strong-recovery-update-error}
    b(x)
    &\leqslant\frac{|\mathcal{I}(x)|}{N}+\exp\bigl(-c_k\beta\lambda m(x)^{k-1}\bigr)\nonumber\\
    &\leqslant\frac{C_k}{\lambda^2m(x)^{2k-2}}
        +\exp\bigl(-c_k\beta\lambda m(x)^{k-1}\bigr).
\end{align}
We observe that the right-hand side of \cref{eq:strong-recovery-update-error} is nonincreasing in $m(x)$.

We now show that the correlation stays above $C\bar r_N/2$ throughout $[0,T]$. Since $s<\nicefrac{1}{2}$, we have $\bar r_N\geqslant N^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}$, and hence $C\bar r_N/2\geqslant4/N$. As the right-hand side of \cref{eq:strong-recovery-update-error} is nonincreasing in $m(x)$ and $\lambda \bar r_N^{k-1}\geqslant C_{\beta,k}$, we obtain the following whenever $m(x)\geqslant C\bar r_N/2$ after adjusting constants appropriately:
\begin{align}
\label{eq:strong-recovery-crude-rate}
    2b(x)
    \leqslant\frac{C_k}{C^{2k-2}C_{\beta,k}^2}
      +2\exp\left(-c_k\beta C^{k-1}C_{\beta,k}\right)
    \leqslant\frac{1}{16}.
\end{align}
Let $\rho\doteq\inf\{t\geqslant0:m(X_t)\leqslant C\bar r_N/2\}$. For $s<\rho$, we have $m(X_s)>C\bar r_N/2$, and hence $2b(X_s)\leqslant\nicefrac{1}{16}$ by \cref{eq:strong-recovery-crude-rate}. Consequently, \cref{eq:strong-recovery-deficit-bound} implies
\begin{align}
\label{eq:strong-recovery-crude-deficit}
    1-m(X_t)
    \leqslant e^{-t}\bigl(1-m(X_0)\bigr)+\bigl(1-e^{-t}\bigr)\frac{1}{16}+7\varepsilon_N,
    \qquad0\leqslant t\leqslant\rho\wedge T.
\end{align}
Since $1-m(X_0)\leqslant1-C\bar r_N$ and $\nicefrac{1}{16}\leqslant1-C\bar r_N$, it follows that
\begin{align*}
    1-m(X_t)\leqslant1-C\bar r_N+7\varepsilon_N<1-\frac{C\bar r_N}{2},
    \qquad0\leqslant t\leqslant\rho\wedge T,
\end{align*}
where the last inequality uses $7\varepsilon_N\leqslant7\bar r_N/32<C\bar r_N/2$. If $\rho\leqslant T$, taking $t=\rho$ contradicts $m(X_\rho)\leqslant C\bar r_N/2$. We conclude that $\rho>T$, so \cref{eq:strong-recovery-crude-deficit} holds for every $t\in[0,T]$.

We next show that the deficit falls below $q_N$ by time $T_{\mathrm{str}}$ and stays below $q_N$ up to time $T$. Since $\lambda^{-2}\leqslant C_{\beta,k}^{-2}N^{-2\gamma}$ with $2\gamma\geqslant\tfrac{k-2}{2}\geqslant\nicefrac{1}{2}>\eta$, and $\exp(-c_k\beta\lambda)=o(N^{-\eta})$, we infer from \cref{eq:strong-recovery-update-error} that, whenever $m(x)\geqslant\nicefrac{1}{3}$,
\begin{align}
\label{eq:strong-recovery-sharp-rate}
    2b(x)\leqslant\frac{C_k}{\lambda^2}+2\exp(-c_k\beta\lambda)\leqslant\frac{q_N}{64}.
\end{align}
By \cref{eq:strong-recovery-crude-deficit},
\begin{align*}
    1-m(X_t)\leqslant e^{-1}+\frac{1}{16}+7\varepsilon_N<\frac23,
    \qquad1\leqslant t\leqslant T,
\end{align*}
where we used $\varepsilon_N=o_N(1)$. Consequently, $m(X_t)>\nicefrac{1}{3}$ for every $t\in[1,T]$. Splitting the integral in \cref{eq:strong-recovery-deficit-bound} at $s=1$, we obtain
\begin{align}
\label{eq:strong-recovery-persistence}
    1-m(X_t)
    &\leqslant e^{-t}\bigl(1-m(X_0)\bigr)
      +\int_0^1e^{-(t-s)}\,2b(X_s)\,ds\nonumber\\
    &\qquad+\int_1^te^{-(t-s)}\,2b(X_s)\,ds
      +7\varepsilon_N\nonumber\\
    &\leqslant e^{-t}
      +\frac{1}{16}\int_0^1e^{-(t-s)}\,ds
      +\frac{q_N}{64}\int_1^te^{-(t-s)}\,ds
      +\frac{7q_N}{32}\nonumber\\
    &\leqslant q_N^2+\frac{q_N^2}{16}+\frac{q_N}{64}+\frac{7q_N}{32}
    <q_N,
    \qquad T_{\mathrm{str}}\leqslant t\leqslant T.
\end{align}
Here, the second inequality uses \cref{eq:strong-recovery-crude-rate,eq:strong-recovery-sharp-rate}.

By \cref{eq:strong-recovery-persistence}, we have $m(X_t)>1-q_N$ for every $t\in[T_{\mathrm{str}},T]$ on $\mathcal{E}\cap\mathcal M$. Taking a union bound and using \cref{eq:strong-recovery-martingale-bound}, we obtain
\begin{align*}
    \mathbb{P}\left[\inf_{t\in[T_{\mathrm{str}},T]}m(X_t)<1-q_N\right]
    \leqslant\mathbb{P}[\mathcal{E}^c]+\mathbb{P}[\mathcal M^c]
    \leqslant\exp(-c_kN)+2\lceil T\rceil\exp\left(-cN\min\{\bar r_N^2,q_N^2\}\right).
\end{align*}
Since $T\leqslant\operatorname{poly}(N)$ and $N\min\{\bar r_N^2,q_N^2\}/\log N\to\infty$ for $s,\eta<\nicefrac{1}{2}$, the proof is concluded by absorbing the factor $\lceil T\rceil$ after adjusting constants appropriately.

\label{cor:averaged-glauber-persistent-strong-recovery}

Suppose $\gamma\geqslant\tfrac{k-2}{4}+\tfrac{1}{4k}$. Fix $\vartheta\in(0,1)$ and $\eta\in(0,\nicefrac{1}{2})$. Let $(X_t)_{t\geqslant0}$ denote the trajectory of the second phase of \cref{alg:averaged-annealed-glauber}, initialized at $\widetilde{X}_0\sim\pi$ with inverse temperatures $\beta_0$ and $\beta$ for the two respective phases, where $\beta_0$ and $T_{\mathrm{av}}$ are chosen as in \cref{thm:tpca-averaged-differential-recovery} for this choice of $\vartheta$. Set $T_{\mathrm{str}}\doteq1+2\eta\log N$. Then, for all sufficiently large $N$ and every deterministic $T$ satisfying $T_{\mathrm{str}}\leqslant T\leqslant\operatorname{poly}(N)$,

\begin{align*}
    \mathbb{P}\left[\inf_{t\in[T_{\mathrm{str}},T]}m(X_t)\geqslant1-N^{-\eta}\right]
    \geqslant1-\exp\left(-c_kN^{\min\{(1-\vartheta)/2,\,1-2\eta\}}\right).
\end{align*}

Define $a\doteq\tfrac{k-1}{4k}$ and note that $\gamma-\tfrac{k-1}{4}\geqslant-a$, so that $\lambda\sigma_N\gtrsim_kC_{\beta,k}N^{-a}$. Combining \cref{thm:tpca-averaged-differential-recovery} with \cref{cor:avg-discrete-grad-correlation}, we obtain the following with probability at least $1-\exp(-c_kN^{(1-\vartheta)/2})$, for sufficiently large $N$ and after choosing $C_{\beta,k}$ sufficiently large:
\begin{align*}
    m(X_0)
    &\geqslant m(\operatorname{sgn}(u_H))-\frac{2}{N}d_H\bigl(X_0,\operatorname{sgn}(u_H)\bigr)\\
    &\geqslant c_k\min\{1,\lambda\sigma_N\}-2\min\{N^{-\vartheta},\lambda^2\sigma_N^2N^{-\vartheta/2}\}\\
    &\geqslant\frac{c_k}{2}\min\{1,\lambda\sigma_N\}
    \geqslant 2N^{-a}.
\end{align*}
For the third inequality, we use $\lambda^2\sigma_N^2N^{-\vartheta/2}\leqslant\lambda\sigma_NN^{-\vartheta/2}$ when $\lambda\sigma_N\leqslant1$, and $N^{-\vartheta}=o(1)$ when $\lambda\sigma_N>1$.

By construction, $(X_t)_{t\geqslant0}$ is Glauber dynamics for $H$ at inverse temperature $\beta$, started from $X_0=\operatorname{sgn}(\bar{X}_{T_{\mathrm{av}}})$. As \cref{thm:strong-recovery-after-critical-correlation} allows the initial state to depend on $H$, we may apply it to $(X_t)_{t\geqslant0}$ with $s=a$ and $C=1$. Since $\gamma\geqslant(k-1)a$, the correlation level $\bar r_N=N^{-a}$ satisfies $\lambda\bar r_N^{k-1}\geqslant C_{\beta,k}$, and $\tau_{\bar r_N}=0$ on the above event. Finally, since $1-2a=\tfrac{k+1}{2k}>\nicefrac{1}{2}>\tfrac{1-\vartheta}{2}$, the proof is concluded via a union bound after adjusting constants appropriately.

\label{thm:critical-recovery-warm-start}
Suppose $\tfrac{k-2}{4}\leqslant\gamma\leqslant\tfrac{k-2}{4}+\tfrac{1}{4k}$. Fix $\vartheta\in(0,\nicefrac{1}{2})$, and let $u_H=\mathbb{E}_\pi[\nabla_\Omega H]$. For every $e\in\Omega_N$, define the stopping time
\begin{align*}
    T_*(e)\doteq\inf\bigl\{t\geqslant0:m(X_t(e))\geqslant Cr_N\bigr\}.
\end{align*}
Then, the following holds for all sufficiently large $N$:
\begin{align*}
    \mathbb{P}\left[T_*(e)\leqslant C_1N^{-(k-1)\left(\gamma-\tfrac{k-2}{4}\right)}
    \ \ \forall\,e\in\mathcal{B}_H\bigl(\operatorname{sgn}(u_H),\lambda^2\sigma_N^2N^{-\vartheta}\bigr)\right]
    \geqslant1-\exp\bigl(-cN\lambda^2\sigma_N^2\bigr),
\end{align*}
where $C_1,c>0$ are constants depending only on $\beta$, $k$ and $C$.

\label{lem:kspin-gaussian-conditioning}
Let $k\geqslant3$ be odd, and let $\zeta\doteq\mathbb{E}_\pi[\nabla_\Omega H_0]$. Then, $H_0(x)=\left\langle \zeta, x \right\rangle+H_0^\perp(x)$, where $H_0^\perp:\Omega_N\to\mathbb{R}$ is a centered Gaussian process independent of $\zeta$ with
\begin{align*}
    \mathbb{E}\bigl[H_0^\perp(x)H_0^\perp(y)\bigr]
    =N\left(\frac{\left\langle x, y \right\rangle}{N}\right)^k-\sigma_N^2\left\langle x, y \right\rangle.
\end{align*}

\label{lem:tpca-hamiltonian-under-gauge}
Let $m_\phi(y)\doteq m(x^{\star}\odot\phi\odot y)$ and $H_\phi(y)\doteq H(x^{\star}\odot\phi\odot y)$ denote the gauge-transformed correlation and the gauge-transformed tensor PCA Hamiltonian, respectively. Then, $m_\phi(y)=\tfrac{1}{N}\left\langle \phi, y \right\rangle$ and $H_\phi=H_{\mathrm{null}}+H_{\mathrm{sig}}$, where
\begin{align*}
    H_{\mathrm{null}}(y)
    &\doteq\left\langle R, y \right\rangle+W(y),
    \qquad
    W(y)\doteq H_0^\perp(x^{\star}\odot\phi\odot y),\\
    H_{\mathrm{sig}}(y)
    &\doteq N\lambda m_\phi(y)^k-N\lambda\sigma_N^2m_\phi(y).
\end{align*}
Moreover, the following hold:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item The Gaussian process $W$ is independent of $(\phi,R)$ and has the same law as $H_0^\perp$.
    \item For any $e\in\Omega_N$, the trajectory $Y_t(e)$ has the same law as Glauber dynamics for the gauge-transformed Hamiltonian $H_\phi$ at inverse temperature $\beta$, initialized at $Y_0=e$.
\end{enumerate}

\label{def:planted-law}
The planted law $\mathbb{P}$ is the law of Glauber dynamics in a random environment, which factorizes as $\mathbb{P}=\mathbb{P}_{\mathrm{env}}(\phi,R,W)\cdot\mathbb{P}_{\mathrm{dyn}}(\cdot\mid\phi,R,W)$, where $\mathbb{P}_{\mathrm{env}}$ and $\mathbb{P}_{\mathrm{dyn}}$ are defined as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $(\phi_i,R_i)=(\operatorname{sgn}(U_i),\abs{U_i})$, where $U_i\overset{\mathrm{i.i.d.}}{\sim}\mathcal{N}(\lambda\sigma_N^2,\sigma_N^2)$ for $i\in[N]$.
    \item $W$ is independent of $(\phi,R)$ and has the same law as $H_0^\perp$.
    \item Conditional on the environment $(\phi,R,W)$, $\mathbb{P}_{\mathrm{dyn}}$ is the law of Glauber dynamics at inverse temperature $\beta$ for the Hamiltonian $H_\phi(y)=\left\langle R, y \right\rangle+W(y)+N\lambda m_\phi(y)^k-N\lambda\sigma_N^2m_\phi(y)$, initialized at $Y_0=e$. Its generator is $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{pl}}$.
\end{enumerate}

\label{def:null-law}
The null law $\mathbb{Q}$ is the law of Glauber dynamics in a random environment, which factorizes as $\mathbb{Q}=\mathbb{Q}_{\mathrm{env}}(\phi,R,W)\cdot\mathbb{Q}_{\mathrm{dyn}}(\cdot\mid\phi,R,W)$, where $\mathbb{Q}_{\mathrm{env}}$ and $\mathbb{Q}_{\mathrm{dyn}}$ are defined as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $\phi_i\overset{\mathrm{i.i.d.}}{\sim}\operatorname{Uniform}(\{\pm1\})$ and $R_i=\abs{U_i}$, where $U_i\overset{\mathrm{i.i.d.}}{\sim}\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_N^2)$ for $i\in[N]$, independently of $\phi$.
    \item $W$ is independent of $(\phi,R)$ and has the same law as $H_0^\perp$.
    \item Conditional on the environment $(\phi,R,W)$, $\mathbb{Q}_{\mathrm{dyn}}$ is the law of Glauber dynamics at inverse temperature $\beta$ for the Hamiltonian $H_{\mathrm{null}}(y)=\left\langle R, y \right\rangle+W(y)$, initialized at $Y_0=e$. Its generator is $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{null}}$.
\end{enumerate}

\label{lem:null-trajectory-uniform-concentration}
For every $T>0$ and $e\in\Omega_N$,
\begin{align*}
    \mathbb{E}_{\mathbb{Q}}\left[\exp\left(\frac{N}{16}\sup_{t\in[0,T]}\bigl|(\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{null}}m_\phi)(Y_t(e))\bigr|^2\right)\right]
    \leqslant\sqrt2(1+NT).
\end{align*}

Throughout, we work under $\mathbb{Q}$ and write $Y_t=Y_t(e)$. Let $(\mathcal{F}_t)_{0\leqslant t\leqslant T}$ denote the filtration of Glauber dynamics up to time $T$ defined in \cref{subsec:cts-glauber}. By \cref{def:null-law}, conditional on $R,W,\mathcal{F}_T$, the coordinates of $\phi$ remain i.i.d.\ Rademacher variables, and the trajectory $(Y_t)_{0\leqslant t\leqslant T}$ is $\mathcal{F}_T$-measurable. Moreover, by \cref{eq:glauber-generator},
\begin{align}
\label{eq:null-drift-rademacher-sum}
    (\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{null}}m_\phi)(y)
    =\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^N\phi_i\eta_i(y),
    \qquad
    \eta_i(y)\doteq\tanh\bigl(\beta\partial_iH_{\mathrm{null}}(y)\bigr)-y_i.
\end{align}
Fix an $\mathcal{F}_T$-measurable random state $y \in \Omega_N$. Conditional on $R,W,\mathcal{F}_T$, the coefficients $\eta_i(y)$ are fixed with $\abs{\eta_i(y)}\leqslant2$ and $\phi_i\overset{\mathrm{i.i.d.}}{\sim}\operatorname{Uniform}(\{\pm1\})$. Hence, \cref{eq:null-drift-rademacher-sum} is a Rademacher sum with variance proxy $4/N$. Hence, by~\citet[Theorem~2.6]{wainwright2019high}, we obtain the following:
\begin{align}
\label{eq:null-drift-conditional-mgf}
    \mathbb{E}_{\mathbb{Q}}\left[\exp\left(\frac{N}{16}(\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{null}}m_\phi)(y)^2\right)\,\middle|\,R,W,\mathcal{F}_T\right]
    \leqslant\sqrt2.
\end{align}

Recall from \cref{subsec:cts-glauber} that $N_T\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(NT)$ denotes the number of arrivals up to time $T$. Since the trajectory is piecewise constant, it visits at most $N_T+1$ states during $[0,T]$, each of which is $\mathcal{F}_T$-measurable. Bounding the supremum of the exponential by the sum over these states, we infer from \cref{eq:null-drift-conditional-mgf} that
\begin{align*}
    \mathbb{E}_{\mathbb{Q}}\left[\exp\left(\frac{N}{16}\sup_{t\in[0,T]}\bigl|(\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{null}}m_\phi)(Y_t)\bigr|^2\right)\,\middle|\,R,W,\mathcal{F}_T\right]
    \leqslant\sqrt2(N_T+1).
\end{align*}
The proof is concluded by taking expectations and using $\mathbb{E}[N_T]=NT$.

