By \cref{lem:kspin-sobolev-norm-concentration}, we can choose $C_1$ such that $\mathbb{P}[\mathcal{E}]\geqslant1-\exp(-c_kN)$. Henceforth, we work on $\mathcal{E}$.

We first show that each update from a state $x$ with $\widetilde m(x)\geqslant\rho$ sets the updated spin to its value in $\widetilde x^\star$ with high probability. For $i\in[N]$, define $\widetilde m_{-i}(x)\doteq N^{-1}\sum_{j\ne i}\widetilde x^\star_jx_j$. If $\widetilde m(x)\geqslant4/N$, then $\widetilde m_{-i}(x)\pm1/N\geqslant\widetilde m(x)-2/N\geqslant\widetilde m(x)/2$. Hence, repeating the steps in \cref{eq:strong-recovery-signal-field} in the proof of \cref{thm:strong-recovery-after-critical-correlation} with $\widetilde x^\star$ and $\widetilde m$, we obtain
\begin{align}
\label{eq:weak-to-strong-signal-field}
    N\lambda\widetilde x^\star_i\partial_i(\widetilde m^k)(x)
    =\frac{N\lambda}{2}\left[
        \left(\widetilde m_{-i}(x)+\frac1N\right)^k
        -\left(\widetilde m_{-i}(x)-\frac1N\right)^k
    \right]
    \geqslant k\lambda\left(\frac{\widetilde m(x)}{2}\right)^{k-1}.
\end{align}
Since $k\geqslant4$, we have $\lambda\geqslant C_{\beta,k,\rho}N^{\tfrac{k-2}{4}}\geqslant C_{\beta,k,\rho}\sqrt N$. Hence, the following holds on $\mathcal{E}$ after setting $C_{\beta,k,\rho}$ to be a sufficiently large constant:
\begin{align}
\label{eq:weak-to-strong-disorder-bound}
