Since the proof of \cite[Lemma~5.1]{arous2020algorithmic} assumes continuity of $f$, we present a short proof for bounded measurable $f$ for completeness. Define
\begin{align*}
    g(t)\doteq a+c\int_0^tf(s)^{k-1}\,ds.
\end{align*}
Since $k$ is odd, we have $f(t)\geqslant g(t)\geqslant a>0$ for every $t\in[0,T]$. Moreover, since $f$ is bounded and measurable, $g$ is absolutely continuous, and for almost every $t\in[0,T]$,
\begin{align*}
    g'(t)=cf(t)^{k-1}\geqslant cg(t)^{k-1}.
\end{align*}
Since $g$ is bounded away from zero, $g^{-(k-2)}$ is also absolutely continuous, and the following holds for almost every $t\in[0,T]$:
\begin{align*}
    \frac{d}{dt}g(t)^{-(k-2)}
    =-(k-2)g(t)^{-(k-1)}g'(t)
    \leqslant-(k-2)c.
\end{align*}
Integrating and using $g(0)=a$, we obtain
\begin{align*}
    g(t)^{-(k-2)}\leqslant a^{-(k-2)}-(k-2)ct.
\end{align*}
If $(k-2)ca^{k-2}t<1$, the right-hand side is positive, and raising both sides to the power $-\frac{1}{k-2}$ yields
\begin{align*}
    g(t)\geqslant\bigl[a^{-(k-2)}-(k-2)ct\bigr]^{-\frac{1}{k-2}}.
\end{align*}
The proof is concluded by noting that $f(t)\geqslant g(t)$.
